"""Per-gene background prose for Panel C (neurology/neurodegeneration). Results-independent."""

GENE_BG_C = {
"APP": "Amyloid precursor protein is the substrate of the Alzheimer cascade: sequential cleavage by beta- and gamma-secretase releases amyloid-beta, whose aggregation defines the disease's plaques. APP's normal function involves synaptic adhesion and signalling, functions old enough that homologs exist across animals, including in species with no amyloid pathology; the disease is a derived malfunction layered on an ancient protein.",
"PSEN1": "Presenilin-1 is the catalytic subunit of gamma-secretase, the intramembrane protease that finishes the amyloid-beta cut. Its mutations cause the majority of early-onset familial Alzheimer disease. Presenilins are among the oldest components of metazoan signalling, predating nervous systems, which makes their evolutionary mapping particularly informative about what the protein was for before there were brains to degenerate.",
"PSEN2": "Presenilin-2 is PSEN1's paralog, a rarer cause of familial Alzheimer disease with partially redundant function. The pair bracket the question of paralog buffering: where one presenilin is present in a genome, the other's absence marks a lineage-specific simplification of an originally two-gene system.",
"MAPT": "MAPT encodes tau, the microtubule-associated protein whose aggregation defines tauopathies including frontotemporal dementia and the tangles of Alzheimer disease. Tau's normal role in axonal transport is tied to long axons and complex neural architectures, so its phylogenetic distribution is expected to track nervous-system elaboration; a cnidarian absence would be expected and informative.",
"SNCA": "Alpha-synuclein is the aggregating protein of Lewy bodies in Parkinson disease and multiple system atrophy. Its normal function at synaptic vesicle pools is vertebrate-flavoured, and clear orthologs outside vertebrates are contested, making it a test case for how far back synaptic maintenance genes go.",
"LRRK2": "LRRK2 is a large multidomain kinase whose hyperactivating mutations are the most common genetic cause of Parkinson disease. It regulates membrane trafficking and lysosomal function. LRRK-family kinases are ancient, with homologs in organisms lacking neurons entirely, so mapping distinguishes the ancient scaffold from the vertebrate disease surface.",
"PARK7": "PARK7 encodes DJ-1, a redox-sensing protein whose loss causes early-onset Parkinson disease. DJ-1 protects against oxidative stress, a function as old as aerobic life, and its deep conservation makes it one of the panel's likeliest ancient finds and a direct bridge between redox maintenance and dopaminergic survival.",
"PINK1": "PINK1 is the mitochondrial-import-status kinase that initiates mitophagy with Parkin; its loss causes early-onset Parkinson disease. Mitochondrial quality control is universal in eukaryotes, but the PINK1/Parkin module's distribution is uneven, so its mapping traces when neurons acquired this particular surveillance system.",
"PRNP": "The prion protein is the substrate of Creutzfeldt-Jakob and related transmissible spongiform diseases. Its normal function remains debated, involving copper handling and synaptic signalling. PRNP is a vertebrate gene; its expected absence in cnidarians marks prion disease as a vertebrate-specific vulnerability.",
"GBA": "GBA encodes glucocerebrosidase, the lysosomal enzyme deficient in Gaucher disease; carriers of GBA mutations have markedly elevated Parkinson risk, tying lysosomal storage biology to synucleinopathy. Lysosomal enzymes are ancient, and GBA's mapping tests the depth of the lysosome-neurodegeneration axis.",
"HTT": "Huntingtin is a giant scaffold protein whose polyglutamine expansion causes Huntington disease, the archetypal repeat-expansion disorder. Normal huntingtin functions in vesicle transport and transcriptional support, and huntingtin homologs with stripped-down glutamine content exist in invertebrates, letting the map separate the ancient scaffold from the expansion-prone region.",
"SOD1": "SOD1, copper-zinc superoxide dismutase, was the first discovered ALS gene. It is universal antioxidant machinery present from bacteria to humans, so its presence in the jellyfish is the panel's positive-control-like expectation; the interesting variable is whether a rejuvenating animal shows anything unusual in its handling.",
"TARDBP": "TARDBP encodes TDP-43, the RNA-binding protein whose cytoplasmic mislocalisation and aggregation characterises most ALS and much frontotemporal dementia. TDP-43's normal role in RNA processing is conserved across animals, and its cnidarian homologs are known, making it a strong candidate for the ancient-maintenance class of neurodegeneration genes.",
"FUS": "FUS is another aggregating RNA-binding protein in ALS and frontotemporal dementia, functionally adjacent to TDP-43 in RNA metabolism. Like TARDBP it belongs to the ancient ribonucleoprotein machinery, and the pair test whether RNA-quality-control proteins are a coherent ancient module in the jellyfish.",
"C9orf72": "The C9orf72 hexanucleotide repeat expansion is the most common inherited cause of ALS and frontotemporal dementia; the gene's normal function runs through autophagy and lysosomal regulation and small GTPase control. C9orf72 homologs exist outside vertebrates, so its mapping adds a trafficking-and-clearance gene to the ancient set.",
"GRN": "GRN encodes progranulin, a secreted growth-factor-like protein whose haploinsufficiency causes frontotemporal dementia; it modulates lysosomal function and neuroinflammation. Granulin-domain proteins have a patchy phylogenetic distribution, so its presence or absence is genuinely uncertain in advance.",
"SQSTM1": "SQSTM1 encodes p62, the autophagy adaptor that delivers ubiquitinated cargo to autophagosomes; its mutations cause ALS, frontotemporal dementia, and Paget disease of bone. p62 sits at the exact intersection of proteostasis and neurodegeneration, and its core domains are old, making it a prime ancient-maintenance candidate.",
"OPTN": "Optineurin is an autophagy receptor mutated in ALS and primary open-angle glaucoma, linking selective autophagy to both motor-neuron and retinal ganglion-cell survival. Its domain architecture is conserved across animals, and its mapping adds the receptor layer of selective autophagy to the jellyfish census.",
"VCP": "VCP, also known as p97, is an AAA-ATPase that extracts ubiquitylated proteins from complexes and membranes for degradation; its mutations cause multisystem proteinopathy spanning muscle, bone, and frontotemporal degeneration. VCP is among the most conserved proteins in eukaryotes and a certain presence; divergence, not presence, is its informative readout.",
"UBQLN2": "UBQLN2 is a ubiquitin-shuttling factor for proteasomal and autophagic degradation whose mutations cause ALS with dementia. Ubiquilins have a broad but uneven phylogenetic footprint, so UBQLN2 helps calibrate which quality-control branches predate the vertebrate nervous system.",
"TREM2": "TREM2 is the microglial receptor whose variants raise Alzheimer risk severalfold, the central gene of the neuroimmune axis of neurodegeneration. It is a vertebrate immune-receptor innovation and is expected to be absent from cnidarians, marking the neuroimmune axis as a derived vulnerability layered onto ancient maintenance cores.",
"BDNF": "Brain-derived neurotrophic factor is the most studied neurotrophin, sustaining neuronal survival, synaptic plasticity, and memory; its decline tracks cognitive aging, and its supplementation is a perennial therapeutic hope. Neurotrophin-family signalling has cnidarian precursors, so BDNF's mapping tests how old trophic support of neurons really is.",
"NGF": "Nerve growth factor, the founding neurotrophin, sustains cholinergic and sensory neurons and is lost early in Alzheimer-relevant circuits. Like BDNF it belongs to a family with deep metazoan precursors, and the pair jointly test the antiquity of trophic neuronal maintenance.",
"APOE": "Apolipoprotein E is shared with Panel B: the major common genetic axis of both longevity and Alzheimer risk, and a vertebrate lipid-transport innovation expected to be absent from cnidarians, where it serves as the contrast case defining the derived edge of the aging-neurology interface.",
}
